# Staged specimen bitstreams live in Git LFS. A published staging is 184 files and
# 365.7 MiB; committed as ordinary blobs that is permanent repository weight, and unlike
# almost every other mistake in this pipeline a later commit does not undo it.
#
# Deliberately narrow. Only staged bitstreams match: the manifest, the attestations, the
# prediction commitment and every certificate stay ordinary Git files, because they are
# what a reviewer reads and diffs, and `gate_measure_ff.lfs_pointer_problems()` refuses a
# run where any of them turns out to be a pointer.
#
# Two gates enforce this, because a rule nobody checks is a comment:
#   * `scripts/gate_stage_ff_formal.py` refuses to stage unless every path it is about to
#     write resolves the way this file says, in HEAD and in the working tree both;
#   * `scripts/gate_publish_ff_staging.py` is the one that decides what enters history.
#     Run it between `git add` and `git commit`: it reads the index and refuses unless
#     this file is present unchanged in the index — a publication commit does not
#     stage policy — every path resolves under that copy of it, and
#     every bitstream blob is an LFS pointer whose oid is the manifest's pin. The stager
#     alone cannot promise that — `git add` happens after it has finished.
staging/**/*.bit filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
